Third-party material redistributed in this repository
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

corpus/bugs.yaml

    Contains 93 short verbatim excerpts, spanning all 61 entries, from public
    forum posts, issue trackers, mailing lists, blog postmortems and vendor
    errata. They sit principally in the
    `evidence:` field, and in a handful of cases in `symptom:` or
    `root_cause:` where the source's own wording is the finding. Most excerpts
    are a sentence or two; six of the 93 are shorter fragments, the briefest
    being a single word (`"off"` in BUG-0008). Each is attributed to a
    specific public URL in the same entry's
    `source:` block, and is included so that a reader can check that the entry
    describes the bug its source describes.

    The count is 83 in `evidence:`, 8 in `symptom:` and 2 in `root_cause:`.
    An earlier revision published 83 -- the `evidence:`-only figure -- while
    the same sentence named the other two fields, so the number understated
    the third-party content by 11%. Reproduce with a count of quoted spans of
    three or more characters over those three fields of corpus/bugs.yaml.

    These excerpts are NOT licensed under Apache-2.0 and are not the project's
    to relicense. They are quoted for identification, criticism and research;
    copyright in each remains with its author. Anyone redistributing this
    corpus should satisfy themselves on that basis independently.

    The corpus records a source URL for every entry. It does not record a
    checksum or a per-source licence determination, which AGENTS.md asks for
    when redistributing vendor artifacts. That gap is real, stated here
    rather than papered over, and NOT currently tracked in a dedicated
    issue: two earlier revisions claimed it was tracked, each against a
    pointer that did not resolve, and a third revision managed to claim
    both at once (round 18).

parts/examples/*.part.json

    Electrical values transcribed from manufacturer datasheets, with the
    document and revision recorded per field in each record's `provenance`
    block, and a table or section for nearly all of them. Provenance is keyed
    by JSON-pointer prefix with a record-level default, and the count below is
    of values whose governing entry names no table -- today that means falling
    to a default, since every more specific entry carries one, but the gate
    reads the locator field rather than trusting that to stay true: 5 of the
    132 measured values across the five records, of which 4 are ratings.

    Those three numbers are COMPUTED AND GATED by parts/lint-part-data.py, not
    maintained by hand. Three successive revisions of this sentence were wrong
    -- a table-per-field claim, a generalisation of one record's shape to all
    five, and a count that was internally unsatisfiable -- and the third
    carried a recount procedure that did not reproduce it. Change the records
    and the gate will tell you the new numbers.

    Individual numbers are facts and are not themselves
    copyrightable; the datasheets they were read from are not redistributed and
    are not included here. Each record declares a `license_class` describing
    the terms the underlying document was published under -- a vocabulary
    separate from this repository's own licence, defined in
    parts/part-data.schema.json.

